Z||000038||ray b||05-15-2001||09:30 PM||||the alcoa 88 fiero is the best lightest most powerfull factory made street legal fiero<BR>ever made by GM .how and why is that not on topic of HOLY GRAIL<BR>at least one car is private owned and 2 more are of unknown fate unlike the real pace car's that arenot missing or going to be sold<BR>anytime soon.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000039||Oreif||05-15-2001||11:11 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>the alcoa 88 fiero is the best lightest most powerfull factory made street legal fiero<BR>ever made by GM .how and why is that not on topic of HOLY GRAIL<BR>at least one car is private owned and 2 more are of unknown fate unlike the real pace car's that arenot missing or going to be sold<BR>anytime soon.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have been "into" Fiero's since they first came out in 1984 and I have never heard of an 1988 "Alcoa" All aluminum Fiero. I find it hard to believe that GM made them. Especially since the last "Alcoa" aluminum engine was in 1986 on the 4 prototype Grand Prix 2+2 cars. The only time Alcoa had anything to do with a Fiero was when they tried to use an Alcoa 1.8L 4-cyl in the 1984 Fiero. Which was decided not to be used in the design phase. (They did use them in the Sunbird/Cavaliers.)<BR>The last time an Alcoa "All-Aluminum" car was made for Pontiac was a 1942 2-door streamliner. Alcoa then only made engines and parts for engines (Manifolds and brackets) thru the rest of the years.<BR>There is no such car as an 88 Alcoa all aluminum Fiero. It is not listed in the Pontiac History (Which goes up to 1996), It is not listed on  <A HREF="http://www.pontiacpower.net/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.pontiacpower.net/</A> <BR>Which has all the concept/experimental history of Pontiac from the 40's to present day. I also find it strange that Gary W's book on the Fiero, and the "History of the Fiero" book sold by <I>The Fiero Store</I> (which even covers the design of the 89 Fiero cars, all the fiero concept cars, and all Fiero race cars made by Huffaker and Entec/Spice) make no mention of this mythical car. Next your going to tell us it used a Lotus front suspension with a Porsche rear suspension and did 0 to 60 in under 3.0 seconds!<BR> Again you are spouting things that just are not true and with a little effort can be disproved easily. <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">    <BR>Got Emblems?<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000040||Oreif||05-15-2001||11:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>FYI<P> Orief, in Ohio at least it can be done with a car titled as race car. I had old NASCAR Hooters T-bird. I had personalized tags saying HOOTERS and drove it to car shows and speedway season openings. Lisas seen it.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Cool now all we have to do is get enough money to buy one of the REAL pace cars!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000041||Standard||05-15-2001||11:43 PM||||Actually, the alcoa prototypes had an all aluminum frame, which cut the frame weight by something like 30%.  There were two prototypes built with aluminum frames, one with a quad 4 and one with a turbo 3.1 v6(i think).   I sure wouldn't mind getting my hands on one of those...||||reg||1
Z||000042||ray b||05-16-2001||12:02 AM||||ALCAN NOT ALCOA MADE THE ALL ALLOY FIERO<BR>sorry about the wrong alpha-bit soup<BR>but the car is real and out there somewhere<BR>see [topic:Weight Complaint] in chat archive<BR>it is in the Brooklands Fiero Book also.<BR>and a member of this fine forum said he saw one in a private collection not long ago.<BR>so are we on topic NOW MR O????<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000043||Jay||05-16-2001||12:21 AM||||"Perhaps if we build a larg wooden badger...!" <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000044||Oreif||05-16-2001||12:39 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>ALCAN NOT ALCOA MADE THE ALL ALLOY FIERO<BR>sorry about the wrong alpha-bit soup<BR>but the car is real and out there somewhere<BR>see [topic:Weight Complaint] in chat archive<BR>it is in the Brooklands Fiero Book also.<BR>and a member of this fine forum said he saw one in a private collection not long ago.<BR>so are we on topic NOW MR O????<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>OK, I'll admit you are on topic, but your facts are still way off. I looked thru everything, and as Standard mentioned above there was 2 prototype aluminum "framed" Fiero's. (not 3 and the book does not state Alcoa made them but they may have.) Per the Fiero History, both were 88GT's with Quad4 engines. They were tested in the summer of 1987. The cars were not "All-Aluminum" just the space frame was. (not sure if the Quad4 is an aluminum engine.) But these are not street legal cars. (saftey and emissions are not to spec's, aluminum alloy frame would most likely fail crash tests) They are classified as prototypes. Just like the 89 Fiero's. If someone here has seen one it was not in a "private garage". GM allows Exec's to trailer cars to shows. During a Michigan Fiero club event, A GM Pontiac exec trailered the Fiero 2+2 (4-seater) to a show.<BR>Prototypes cannot be sold or registered for street use as a normal car. As for which is faster, I'll bet the SD4's were faster than the Quad4 cars even with the reduced weight. The weight savings was only 500 lbs. There is almost 70hp difference between the pace car SD4's and the Quad4's. <P>Quad4 in aluminum Fiero GT had weight to HP ratio of 13.4 lbs for every HP.<BR>The SD4 pace car had 10.2 lbs for every HP.<BR>Nomal production V-6 Fiero is 18.9 lbs for every HP.<BR>Normal 2.5L Fiero coupe 26.6 lbs for every HP.<BR>IMSA Fiero race cars had 7.1 lbs for every HP.<P>Anyone can tell you which would win, The less weight per HP would.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000045||87ECO5||05-16-2001||08:47 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>Normal 2.5L Fiero coupe 26.6 lbs for every HP.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>WHOA!  Wait a minute, Oreif!  Is that the 92 horsepower Duke or the 98 horsepower duke!?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000046||Oreif||05-16-2001||09:38 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87ECO5:<BR><B> WHOA!  Wait a minute, Oreif!  Is that the 92 horsepower Duke or the 98 horsepower duke!?     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That is the 92HP. I used a 1984 coupe as the reference. The 98HP is 25 lbs for every HP.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
