Z||000056||Fiero5||05-16-2001||01:32 PM||||The Tale of Sir Launcelot.<BR>Father:<BR>One day, lad, all this will be yours!<BR>Prince Herbert:<BR>What, the curtains?<BR>Father:<BR>No. Not the curtains, lad. All that you can see, stretched out over the hills and valleys of this land! All these used Fieros that I purchased from Fiero Dave, lad.<BR>Herbert:<BR>But Mother--<BR>Father:<BR>Father, lad. Father.<BR>Herbert:<BR>B-- b-- but Father, I don't want any of Fiero Daves Fieros.<BR>Father:<BR>Listen, lad. I built some of these Fieros back up from nothing. When I started here, all there was was busted panels, cracked windshields, blown heads, torn aero bumpers and such. Other kings said I was daft to build a wankel engine into a Fiero, but I built it all the same, just to show 'em. It snapped in two in just a few hours. So, I built a second one. That snapped in two. So, I built a third one. That burned up, flipped over, then snapped in two, but the fourth one... stayed running! And that's what you're gonna get, lad. The only running wankel powered Fiero in these islands.<BR>Herbert:<BR>But I don't want any of that. I'd rather--<BR>Father:<BR>Rather what?!<BR>Herbert:<BR>I'd rather...<BR>[music]<BR>...just... sing!<BR><P>------------------<BR>                 <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_2.jpg">               <BR>http://www.kelleyfamilyfieros.homestead.com/index.html||||reg||7
Z||000057||Oreif||05-16-2001||02:16 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>two quad 4's plus at least one 3.1 v6[turbo?]<BR>makes 3 alloy cars unless one of the quads was remotored.yes quad 4 is all alloy block and heads and so was the v-6 block [maybe heads to]. and a member said that one car was in private ownership the how and legal<BR>i don't know but sh*t happens and i am glad it does.IMSA car is steel tube frame race car with SD-4 turned 90* to mate with hewland trans with very little fiero parts if any used in it.i saw it race at miami GP<BR>and lead a pack of v-8 pony cars,a sweet car but not realy a street fiero.and of the street cars i think that a alloy car would beat a sd-4 on a road race or auto-X track with it's lite weight advantage. so it is the HOLY GRAIL OF FIERO's  <P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>There was no 3.1L turbo. 3.1L's were not available until late 1989. These cars were tested in 1987. In 1985 they built 2 2.9L turbo/intercooled aluminum V-6's but these were put into production 85 GT's for testing purposes. They are considered "prototypes". Don't you think that the 88 Fiero's would have had 3.1L engines if they were available? As for the IMSA car, again you are wrong. All the huffaker built IMSA cars had the GM 4-spd with a strenghtened case and Webster performance gears. It was still transverse mounted. The only Fiero's with the engine turned 90* was the drag race car. The GTP Spice/Entac was also transverse mounted. <P>There were a total of 10 prototype Fiero's built by Pontiac. None of them are in the hands of "private parties" All are at Pontiac. There is no way they could be sold or given. Just ask John Delorien (sp?), He tried like crazy to buy or get the "Banshee" prototype that he built for GM back in the early 70's. Don't you think that someone who designed the macho corvette body and provided GM things like the GTO, Firebird, Camaro, Chevelle, etc would have the power to aquire the prototype? (before his "falling out" with the corporate office)<P><BR>By the way ~  *The IMSA fiero at the Miami GP on March 2nd 1986 was driven by Bob Earl in the Goodwrench IMSA car built by Huffaker in November 1985. (Bob Earl won the GTU class in that race) So it was transverse mounted just like a stock Fiero.<P>*Source: Gary Witzenburg's Fiero book. Page 108.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000058||ray b||05-16-2001||05:02 PM||||out the front door with a title maybe not <BR>but who knows what goes out the back door<BR>esp when nobody is looking or cares much<BR>did gm own the alloy cars or did alcan????<BR>were they scraped or ordered to be and saved<BR>by brave man unknown to the big boss???<BR>i sure hope so and have heard of other show cars saved from gm bosses heavy hands by disloyal workers so maybe the 89's will pop up in a few years time no info just hope on this.<BR>more than one imsa was built, with motor 90*<BR>in later cars<BR>don't know where you saw a spice with transverce motor but thats not what i saw at imsa races in GT lites may be post racing street convert?? or cheap fix for blow up parts?? not race speck spice as built up by them.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000059||Raydar||05-16-2001||05:21 PM||||I remember reading an article in the FOCOA (maybe?) magazine. <BR>The article stated that there were actually 4 fastback Formulas manufactured. This was at the end of the 88 production run. Apparently they had four Formulas scheduled for assembly, but ran out of notchback rear clips. The line workers decided to build "hybrids". <BR>When management found out, they were furious, and ordered the cars to be crushed, but supposedly several of the workers said "What are they gonna do?! Fire us?!" and sneaked at least one of them out under cover of darkness.<BR>There were pictures in this article of a Formula with a fastback clip. <BR>Authentic? Depends upon who you believe.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||6
Z||000060||Jaei Sun||05-16-2001||08:04 PM||||Taken from Gary's Fiero book:<P>"...Improvements seemed to be extending into the future as well. One Fiero project involved an expiramental aluminum spaceframe devised by Alcan Automotive Structures of Canada. Alcan, with cooperation from Entech and GM's CPC Group, had the aluminum spaceframe pressed in the same dies and in the same gauges as the Fiero's conventional steel structure. Two Alcan Fieros were built in 1986, both using special bonding adhesives that completely eliminated welding. The aluminem spaceframes weighed 68% less than their steel counterparts. They also boosted torsional stiffness by 42% and bending stiffness by 54%. The Ferrari 408 and the Jaguar XJ220 prototypes used this same aluminum technology.<P>One of the two Alcan Fieros went into durability testing and was driven 36,000 miles at the GM Proving Grounds (equivalent to about 100,000 miles elsewhere) with nary a squeak or rattle. The other was assigned to Pontiac engineer Terry Satchell for further development. Satchell updated the suspension to 1988 specs and dropped an Olds Quad 4 engine into it. He later invited members of the press and GM management to drive this car.<P>Everyone came away awed. The dohc Quad 4, which was easily tweaked to 190 bhp, turned the Fiero into a veritable rocket. Not only that, but the engine compartment and Getrag 5 speed needed no alterations to accept the Quad 4! It dropped right in with the merest rerouting of hoses and wires....."||||reg||1
Z||000061||84Bill||05-16-2001||11:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero5:<BR><B>Other kings said I was daft to build a wankel engine into a Fiero, but I built it all the same, just to show 'em. It snapped in two in just a few hours. So, I built a second one. That snapped in two. So, I built a third one. That burned up, flipped over, then snapped in two, but the fourth one... stayed running! And that's what you're gonna get, lad. The only running wankel powered Fiero in these islands.<BR>Herbert:<BR>But I don't want any of that. I'd rather--<BR>Father:<BR>Rather what?!<BR>Herbert:<BR>I'd rather...<BR>[music]<BR>...just... sing!<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>OMG thats funny! Burned up LOL flipperd over ROFLOL then snapped in two!! ROGPML <P>Stop that! Stop that! we'll ave none of that here.<P>wow man, I think I hurt myself.<BR>that was just way to damn funny<BR>||||reg||10
